      "id": "ML-C62-rope",
      "sequence": 1990,
      "title": "കയർ (kayaṟ) — coir rope",
      "headword": "കയർ",
      "romanization": "kayaṟ",
      "gloss": "coir rope",
      "script": "malayalam",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:267b0fc5f2689019",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did śarkkara mean, and what did muḷakŭ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: കയർ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കയർ (kayaṟ) — \"coir rope\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, from a root about twisting, and it names both the fibre and the rope twisted out of it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The fibre comes off the husk of the coconut you met on the shelf, soaked in backwater until it softens and then beaten out by hand. Kerala has made rope this way for as long as anyone has written about it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English borrowed the word along with the trade: coir is this word, carried out of Malabar on Portuguese and Dutch ships."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കയർ (kayaṟ) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil kayiṟu (DEDR 1254)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five things made by hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
