        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: നൂല്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "നൂല് (nūlŭ) — \"thread\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, matching Tamil nūl, and it stands behind tuṇi, the cloth-word: one is what the other is woven from."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The older language put the same word to a second job. A treatise, a work of scholarship, was also a nūl — a thread of argument running from one end of a subject to the other, which is close to what English does with a train of thought."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It closes on the bare l of cōṟŭ and vērŭ: the consonant with its vowel shaved off."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "നൂല് (nūlŭ) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil nūl, Kannada nūl and Telugu nūlu (DEDR 3726)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two."
            }
          ]
        },
