          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ചൂല് (cūlŭ) — \"a broom\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, and a Kerala ചൂല് (cūlŭ) is a bundle of coconut-leaf ribs bound tight at one end — the coconut again, a third time on a third object."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sweeping the yard at first light is the opening work of a Kerala day, done before the kitchen is lit, and it leaves the ground marked in long strokes that stay until somebody walks on them."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "There is a second broom for indoors, softer, made of grass rather than leaf-rib, and the same word covers it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ചൂല് (cūlŭ) is native Dravidian, from the verb \"to sweep\"; DEDR 2599 lists the root with Tamil cī."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
